<p>As I'm sensing this is going to be a common theme in criticism of my skip, I feel like I'm obligated to address it.</p>
<p>I'd encourage anyone reading this and having the same thoughts regarding 3999 to reread that scp and my own. 3999's author and myself use a similar style (which, I should note, has been for 100+ years and is kind of the zeitgeist of postmodern literature) to very different end goals. We have fundamentally different stories, different statements to make on. If 3999 is Finnegan's Wake, this is House of Leaves. If 3999 is Gravity's Rainbow or Infinite Jest (closer to Pynchon than Wallace due to 3999's abundance of dreams/stream of consciousness) this is theMystery.Doc, or John Dies at the End. I could go on. Was Danielewski aware of and influenced by James Joyce? It certainly seems that way - Joyce undoubtedly pioneered a style of deconstructionist literature, his influence is vast and cannot be understated. But being the first (not that Joyce was, either) does not give one a monopoly on an idea or structure. 3999 is as influenced, at least apparently (I won't speak to the author's intent, but &quot;the author is dead&quot; anyway), by the authors I list in my scp as I am by them and by it. Furthermore, 3999 and 3692 are saying very different things on fiction, identity, emotion, and other concepts. At least in my interpretation of both.</p>
<p>In light of criticism, however, I may take her down and spend some more time working with her - this might be &quot;finished,&quot; but all documents are living - but I still stand by my idea.</p>
<p>So is this &quot;3999 but worse,&quot; then? Maybe. Maybe it needs work to more fully stand on its own on this site. Maybe I'll scrap it and write another abstract&#8230; thing. That's up for y'all to decide. I just find issue with surface similarities in 1/4th of my article seemingly becoming synecdoche for the deeper meaning of this piece as a whole.</p>
<p>Your commentary is obviously valid - what I'm saying has no more inherent worth as an interpretation than your own. I am curious about the typos you mention, however.</p>

