<p>Ahh, understandable. That wasn't necessarily directed straight at you alone, by the way. And 3999 is basically interchangeable with 3005, 3211, etc in my above reply - the point being that this is a style I'm using to my own ends. I'd also like to know if you can think of the skip this more closely resembles!</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;Explanation&nbsp;of&nbsp;concept&nbsp;+&nbsp;reply</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;Hide&nbsp;this&nbsp;mess</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>Regarding your criticism about the suspension of disbelief - it's supposed to be on shaky ground to begin with. Not shattered entirely, so for you at least, I failed there. But clearance Levels 2 and 3 have more things separate from them aside from the blue bits and un-redacted info (&quot;this may hurt&quot; -&gt; &quot;this will hurt&quot;, the computer's inconsistent replies to invalid commands, and a lot other smaller easter eggs) - which are meant to be hints that something isn't right. Then when June relives Jamie's death, that's when they break.</p>
<p>The overarching concept (well, one layer) of this is that June is writing the whole thing, computer terminal and all, in the notebook, in an attempt to cling to their identity and their reality, which fails as real life and other fictions bleed in. They then fade away when I stop writing them/when they stop writing this article (hence &quot;I need to keep writing&quot;). My biggest issue writing this was putting enough hints into the first two articles to build up to the third, without showing my hand too early. Balancing the build-up/escalation with a marginal appearance of normalcy was complicated, to say the least.</p>
</div>
